Consider the class of singular nonlinear elliptic boundary value problems of the form
\[
-\Delta u + g(u) = \lambda f(x,u) \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u \ge 0 \text{ in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \text{ on } \partial\Omega,
\]
where \(\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^N\) (\(N>1\)) is a bounded smooth domain, \(\lambda>0\) is a parameter, \(f\) is a nonnegative source, and \(g:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)\) is a strong absorption term that is singular or non-Lipschitz at \(0\) (a typical model is \(g(u)=u^q\) with \(0<q<1\), possibly with \(g(0)=0\) and \(g'(0^+)=+\infty\)). For such problems, "free boundary" solutions are nonnegative weak solutions whose support \(\{u>0\}\) is strictly contained in \(\Omega\), so that the boundary of the support inside \(\Omega\) is a free boundary; "flat solutions" are those satisfying both \(u=0\) and \(\partial_\nu u=0\) on \(\partial\Omega\). Assume that, for the Laplacian diffusion above, multiplicity results are known: there exist parameter ranges of \(\lambda\) for which the problem admits at least two distinct positive solutions, and/or distinct free boundary solutions, and/or coexistence of a positive solution with a compactly supported (free boundary) solution.

The problem is to determine which of these multiplicity phenomena persist when the diffusion operator \(-\Delta\) is replaced by each of the following, while keeping the same type of singular absorption \(g(u)\) and source \(\lambda f(x,u)\):

(a) a general linear second-order uniformly elliptic operator \(L u = -\sum_{i,j} a_{ij}(x)\partial_{ij} u + \sum_i b_i(x)\partial_i u + c(x) u\) with smooth coefficients;

(b) the \(p\)-Laplacian \(-\Delta_p u = -\operatorname{div}(|\nabla u|^{p-2}\nabla u)\) for \(1<p<\infty\);

(c) fully nonlinear operators of Hamilton–Jacobi type, e.g. \(-\Delta u + H(x,\nabla u)\) with \(H\) of suitable growth in the gradient;

(d) the Monge–Ampère operator \(\det(D^2 u)\) (in the appropriate convex/concave setting);

(e) the bi-Laplacian \(\Delta^2\) with appropriate (Dirichlet or Navier) boundary conditions;

(f) cooperative or competitive nonlinear elliptic systems and Schrödinger systems with singular absorption acting componentwise.

For each of (a)–(f), establish (or disprove) the existence of parameter regimes producing at least two distinct nonnegative solutions, and characterize when free boundary / flat solutions arise alongside strictly positive solutions, in analogy with the Laplacian case.

